# Build context for backend/wealdrelay/Dockerfile. The context is the repository
# root, because the Cargo workspace root lives there.
#
# Everything that is not needed to compile the relay is excluded, both for build
# speed and so that a stray local artifact cannot change the image digest.

.git
.github
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.githooks

# Documentation, specifications and tooling. None of it compiles the relay, and
# a documentation edit must not change the image digest.
scripts
specs
docs
*.md

# The root LICENSE and NOTICE. Apache 2.0 section 4 is served by
# backend/wealdrelay/LICENSE and its NOTICE, which the Dockerfile copies into the
# image explicitly; a second copy at the root is for humans reading the
# repository and must not be a digest input.
LICENSE
NOTICE

# The local test harness. It brings up Postgres, MinIO and Redis for the
# integration tiers and takes no part in the build.
backend/compose

# Local build output. `target` in particular is large and host-specific.
target
**/target
.build
build
node_modules
**/node_modules
*.profraw

# Local state and editor noise. Everything here is host state, and host state
# inside the context is a reproducibility defect rather than merely waste: the
# Dockerfile ends with `COPY . .`, so a file that reaches the context reaches a
# layer and changes the published digest
# (specs/backend/relay/verification.md proof 1).
.weald
.weald-dev
.weald-fixture
.weald-stack
tmp
.DS_Store
*.swp
.env
.env.*
repro
repro-*

# The relay's own deployment bundle. Templates, compose files and the systemd
# unit are shipped to operators, not compiled into the binary, and a documentation
# edit must not change the image digest.
backend/wealdrelay/deploy

# The root `Dockerfile` is a symlink to backend/wealdrelay/Dockerfile. It exists
# because a provider that builds from a git repository looks for a Dockerfile at
# the repository root and offers no way to say otherwise. Excluded from the
# context so the one real file stays the only input to the digest: a symlink in
# the context would hash as a second copy of the same bytes and make the context
# digest depend on how the checkout materialised links.
# Written without a leading slash: Docker strips one and this file's own
# reimplementation in scripts/repro-context-digest.py does not, and the two have
# to agree or the context digest stops describing the context.
Dockerfile

# Python virtualenvs and bytecode, from the helper scripts. A virtualenv churns
# on every use, so its presence moved the context digest while a build was
# running, and `.venv/bin/` holds symlinks, one of them to a Python outside this
# tree. The copy-of-the-context check is what found it, by refusing to build a
# copy that did not hash the same as the original.
**/.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc
